You don't have 7 guaranteed wishes from the castle wand, only 5.  And 
the last one is not available as an emergency wish, so there are only 4 
"easy" wishes guaranteed.  I personally don't think this is too many. 
At the times of NH 3.1.3 when you could still get all your magical 
armour by polypiling, I would have agreed.  But nowadays, I almost 
always need one or two of the wishes to complete my armour kit.

And then there is the old argument by Boudewijn.  The reason for the 
wand to exist with 100% probability is that you may just not be able to 
get an absolutely necessary item by chance, such as candles, or one that 
is at least close to absolutely necessary, such as a ring of levitation 
(or at least boots).  So you may need to burn one wish for something 
"absolutely" necessary.  If you don't have more than one, where is the 
reward for cleaning out the castle?

And finally, I agree also with David's argument that the game should be 
winnable without (too much) help by the RNG.  In fact, I tend to be 
purist in that respect -- I believe that there should be essentially no 
games that are lost due to the RNG.  So there must be a means 
compensating for bad luck (in reality, not game luck) by some guaranteed 
wishes; the number is of course a matter of discussion.

That I'd like the game to be one that can be won by ability rather than 
by luck is one reason why I don't play conducts unless they come up 
naturally (I have done wishless, polypileless and genocideless, but only 
because a cloak of magic protection appeared early in the game) -- they 
make you dependent on the RNG.  If you have to waste 143 characters to 
win one game with a particularly difficult conduct, then much of your 
success is due to random.  Personally, I cannot *really* blame the RNG 
for any one of my lost games.  (I did have bad luck, but there was 
always a modification of behaviour that would have allowed me to escape 
that particular death.)  Of course, I am still far from winning 100% of 
my games, but I am close to 30% and I think this is o.k. for such a 
difficult game.  For me, the challenge is to try and make *every* 
character survive, no matter how pathetic his stats.
